---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import { SITE } from '../config/site.config';
---

<PageLayout
  title="Disclaimer"
  description={`Editorial independence, affiliate disclosure, AI accuracy caveats and the limits of the information published by ${SITE.name}.`}
  lede="Read this before acting on anything published here."
  lastUpdated={new Date('2026-08-02')}
>
  <div class="not-prose mb-8 rounded-lg border border-[var(--color-warning)]/40 bg-[var(--color-warning)]/10 p-4 text-sm text-[var(--text-muted)]">
    <strong class="text-[var(--text)]">Note:</strong> this disclaimer describes how
    {' '}{SITE.name} actually operates. It is written in good faith but it is not legal
    advice.
  </div>

  <h2>Editorial independence</h2>
  <p>
    {SITE.name} decides what to cover on its own. No vendor, sponsor, advertiser or
    affiliate partner has any say over which tools we write about, what we conclude, or
    when we publish. Nobody outside the desk sees an article before it goes live, and there
    are no pre-agreed conclusions.
  </p>
  <p>
    Vendors sometimes provide trial licenses, extended API credits or briefing access so we
    can test a product properly. Accepting access to test something is not payment for
    coverage, it does not oblige us to publish, and it has never changed a rating. Where it
    happens on a specific piece, we say so in that piece.
  </p>

  <h2>No paid placement</h2>
  <p>
    Listings in the <a href="/tools/">AI tool directory</a>, the
    <a href="/agents/">AI agent directory</a> and the
    <a href="/prompts/">prompt library</a> cannot be bought.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>We do not sell inclusion, position, category placement or a "featured" slot.</li>
    <li>We do not sell ratings, badges or awards.</li>
    <li>We do not accept paid guest posts, sponsored articles or link insertions.</li>
    <li>We do not remove a listed weakness because a vendor asked.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    How entries get in and how ratings are decided is documented in the
    <a href="/editorial-policy/">editorial policy</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>Affiliate disclosure</h2>
  <p>
    Some outbound links to tools and services are affiliate links. If you sign up through
    one, we may earn a commission at no additional cost to you. This is one of the ways the
    site is funded, alongside advertising and reader support.
  </p>
  <p>
    Our position on it is simple and it is not negotiable with partners:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>An affiliate relationship never decides whether a tool is covered or listed.</li>
    <li>An affiliate relationship never affects a rating, a ranking or a recommendation.</li>
    <li>
      Tools with no affiliate program at all — including open-source projects and
      self-hosted options — appear and win comparisons on the same terms as everything
      else.
    </li>
    <li>
      If a tool we earn commission on is the wrong choice for a given use case, the article
      says so.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Where a page contains affiliate links, that is disclosed on the page.
  </p>

  <h2>AI content accuracy</h2>
  <p>
    This subject changes faster than almost anything else we could write about. Model
    versions ship monthly, context windows and rate limits get revised, pricing tiers move,
    free plans are withdrawn, and vendors change license terms and data-retention defaults
    without much warning.
  </p>
  <p>
    Assume anything version-specific, price-specific or benchmark-specific has a short shelf
    life. We date every article and update the important ones, but the vendor's own current
    documentation is always the authority. Verify before you commit budget or architecture
    to a decision.
  </p>
  <p>
    Benchmark results depend on the harness, the prompt, the temperature, the tooling and
    the date. Numbers we cite are attributed to their source and are not directly comparable
    across sources. Where we test something ourselves, we describe the setup so you can
    judge how much weight the result deserves.
  </p>

  <h2>AI outputs are not reliable by default</h2>
  <p>
    Large language models fabricate confidently, reproduce bias in their training data, and
    behave differently after a silent vendor-side update. Code, prompts, agent
    configurations and automation workflows published here are starting points, not
    finished products.
  </p>
  <p>
    Test everything in an environment where failure is cheap. Review generated code before
    it runs. Do not put an autonomous agent in front of production systems, customer data,
    payments or anything irreversible without human review and hard limits on what it can
    do. You remain responsible for what your systems do.
  </p>

  <h2>Not professional advice</h2>
  <p>
    Nothing here is professional, legal, financial, tax, medical, employment or security
    advice. Coverage of AI companies, funding rounds and market moves is journalism, not
    investment advice, and should not be treated as a recommendation to buy or sell
    anything. Decisions about compliance, data protection, model licensing and regulatory
    exposure depend on your specific circumstances — engage a qualified practitioner who can
    look at your actual situation.
  </p>

  <h2>No warranty</h2>
  <p>
    Content is provided "as is" without warranty of any kind. An article that was accurate
    on its publication date may not be accurate today. We make no guarantee that a tool will
    perform for you the way it performed for us, or that it will still exist, still be free,
    or still have the same license next quarter.
  </p>

  <h2>Limitation of liability</h2>
  <p>
    To the maximum extent permitted by law, {SITE.organization.legalName} accepts no
    liability for any loss or damage — including data loss, service disruption, wasted spend,
    financial loss or security incidents — arising from use of, or reliance on, anything
    published here, or from your dealings with any third-party tool or vendor we mention.
    See the <a href="/terms/">terms and conditions</a> for the full position.
  </p>

  <h2>Third-party products and trademarks</h2>
  <p>
    Mentions of vendors, models, products and open-source projects are for identification
    and analysis. They do not constitute endorsement, and no affiliation is implied.
    Trademarks and product names belong to their respective owners.
  </p>

  <h2>External links</h2>
  <p>
    We link out to documentation, papers, repositories and vendor sites because they are
    useful. We do not control them and are not responsible for their content, accuracy,
    availability, pricing, privacy practices or security. A link is not an endorsement of
    the destination or of the company behind it.
  </p>

  <h2>Contact</h2>
  <p>
    Questions, corrections or concerns:
    <a href={`mailto:${SITE.organization.email}`}>{SITE.organization.email}</a>, or use the
    <a href="/contact/">contact page</a>.
  </p>
</PageLayout>
